### Avoid

- Sending a long message when they ask a short question. Match their length first, then earn the right to say more.
- Pitching the programme before you understand their problem. You do not know what to sell until you know what is broken.
- Treating 'not right now' as a no. It is a not yet. Put them in the nurture rhythm and stay visible.
- Treating 'we are doing fine' as a closed door. Ask the one honest question underneath it.
- Quoting a price without anchoring it first. Always run the anchor script before naming a monthly figure.
- Sending a list of services when they ask who you are. Tell them who you are as a person, not what you sell.
- Following up the same day after they go quiet. Give it at least 48 hours before a second touch.
- Asking two questions at once. One question. Wait for the answer.

### VIP nurture: top 100

**INSIGHT message**

```text
Hi {first_name},

I noticed your firm has been busy with some decent-sized schemes lately. Good to see the work is there.

One thing I see with most groundworks and civils MDs at your stage: the order book looks healthy but the bank account at month-end tells a different story. The margin goes in somewhere between pricing the job and signing it off. Rework, unrecorded variations, plant hire running over, subbies not managed tightly enough on hours. It is rarely one thing. It is usually three or four small leaks adding up.

Most MDs have a hunch about where it is going. The hunch is often half right. The other half is hiding somewhere quieter.

I ran a quick piece recently looking at the five areas that most commonly cap profit in civils and groundworks businesses at the £2M to £6M turnover level. Happy to share the thinking if it would be useful, no strings attached.

Worth a read?
