- **Name:** The Margin Finder Session
- **Length:** 30 minutes
- **Who it is for:** MDs of groundworks, civils, drainage and allied trades businesses in Yorkshire, turning over £1.5M to £8M, winning decent work but not keeping enough of it
- **What they get:** A straight look at where the margin is going, which of the five profit levers is capping the business right now, and one or two things they can act on before the week is out
- **The promise:** No worksheets. No flannel. No pitch. Just a proper look at the numbers and an honest answer about where to start.

### The invitation

**Invitation**

```text
Hi {first_name},

I'm running a free 30-minute call this month called the Margin Finder Session. It's for MDs of groundworks and civils businesses who are putting decent turnover through but not seeing enough of it in the bank.

In half an hour we look at:

- Where the margin is leaking between pricing the job and finishing it, so you can stop it going out the door on the next scheme
- Which part of the business is capping your profit right now, so you stop spending time on the wrong thing
- What one change would free up the most hours in your week, so you can get back to pricing work instead of sorting problems

No strings. No pitch. No catch. Just a proper conversation about your numbers.

You walk away with a clear view of which bit to fix first and what it is worth fixing. Even if we never speak again, that is useful.

I have five spots this month. If you want one, book here: {booking_link}
```

_Use as an email, LinkedIn DM or direct outreach to an MD in the trade. Keep the three bullets exactly as written. They each move away from a specific pain Steve names and toward what he actually wants._

### The questions

| Area | Question | Red looks like | Green looks like |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Margin visibility | When a job finishes, do you know within a week what margin you actually made compared to what you priced? | He finds out at year-end through the accounts, or not at all. Variations, rework and plant overruns are not tracked back to the job. | Job costing is done within a week of completion. He can compare priced margin to actual margin on every scheme and spot the pattern. |
| Cash and payment | Do you know, right now, what is owed to you, when it is due, and what you owe out and when? | He has a rough idea. Month-end is stressful. Retentions are parked and forgotten. He has been caught short when two main contractors paid late in the same month. | He has a rolling cash forecast, knows exactly what is coming in and when, and chases retentions systematically before they become a problem. |
| Team and delegation | When your contracts manager or foremen hit a problem on site, what happens next? | It lands on his phone. He sorts it because it is quicker. He is still doing half his contracts manager's job and everyone on the crew knows the boss is the one who actually decides. | The team owns its patch. Problems come to him with a suggested solution attached. He is approving, not fixing. |
| Pricing and pipeline | How much time did you spend last week on pricing and winning future work, versus sorting problems that were already on site? | He is firefighting most of the week. Tenders are rushed or missed. He is pricing in his head on the drive between sites and not writing it up properly. | He has protected time for pricing and pipeline. He knows what work is coming in over the next six months and at what margin. |
| Variations and extras | What is your process for recording and claiming variations before a job closes out? | Variations get agreed verbally and written up late, or not at all. By the time the final account is settled, he has lost extras he was owed. | Every variation is recorded in writing at the time, priced, and submitted before the job closes. He rarely loses money he was owed. |
| Profit levers | Do you know which of your five profit levers, leads, conversion, average job value, repeat work and margin, is the weakest right now? | He has never looked at it this way. He knows things are not quite right but cannot say which bit is capping profit most. | He knows which lever is weakest and has a specific action against it. He can see how a 10% move on each one compounds across the business. |
| Owner dependency | If you were off site for two weeks with no phone, what would stop or go wrong? | Quite a lot. The crew would manage the easy stuff but anything complicated would wait for him or get sorted badly. He has not had a proper holiday in years. | The business runs for two weeks without him. His contracts manager handles the sites, the office handles the admin, and he comes back to an update rather than a pile of problems. |
| Personal return | If you worked out your effective hourly rate, including evenings and weekends, would the number reflect the risk and graft you carry? | He has never done the calculation. He suspects the number would not sit well. The hours are long and the reward does not feel proportionate to what he puts in. | He knows his number, it reflects the risk he carries, and it is going in the right direction year on year. He could not articulate the exact figure but it is healthy. |
| Business value | If you decided to step back or sell in the next five years, what would the business be worth without you in it? | Not much. The business depends on him for relationships, decisions and quality control. Without him, the key clients would be nervous and the jobs would struggle. | The business has systems, a leadership layer and client relationships that are not all held personally by him. It has value beyond his presence. |

