### Questions that find the gap

1. What's the turnover looking like this year, and when you look at the bank account at month-end, does it feel like it reflects that?
2. When a job finishes, do you generally know whether it landed the margin you priced - or does it only become clear afterwards, usually when there's not much left?
3. Where does most of your day actually go? If you think about last week, what were you sorting that probably shouldn't have landed on your desk?
4. You've got a contracts manager - how much of his job are you still doing? And when does it land back on you?
5. What does the pipeline look like right now? Do you know what's coming in over the next six months, or are you pricing reactively as things come up?
6. Cash - how does month-end feel? Are you waiting on retentions or main contractor payments regularly, and does it ever get tight?
7. What have you already tried to sort this? What happened?
8. If we were sitting here in twelve months and things were genuinely better - what would that actually look like for you? In the business, and outside it?
9. And personally - what's the cost of this carrying on the way it is? What's it costing you at home, or with your time?
10. What's stopped you sorting this before now?

> **How to use these questions:** Do not rush through them. Ask one, listen properly, and repeat back what they said before moving to the next. The answers they give you are the words you use in the pitch. Write them down.

### Transition

**Transition into the game plan**

```text
Right. So what I'm hearing is this: the turnover's decent, but the margin's going somewhere between pricing the job and finishing it and you can't pin down where. You're still sorting too much yourself because it's quicker than waiting for someone else to do it. And you're not getting the time to price properly or think six months ahead because the day job is swallowing you whole.

Does that feel about right?

[Wait for confirmation.]

OK. Based on what you've told me, let me show you exactly how I'd approach this with you. Three things. I'll walk you through each one and you can ask me anything as we go.
```

### The game plan

1. **Step 1 - Find out where the margin is going.** Before we do anything else, we look at your numbers properly. Not a general conversation about margin - your jobs, your pricing, your costs, your subbies, your plant hire. The BOSS Scorecard takes three minutes and shows us which of the five profit levers is capping your business before session one even starts. Most MDs in the trade have got a margin problem in two or three specific places. Rework they haven't costed properly. Variations that were never written up and never got paid. Plant hire that ran over and nobody flagged it until the job was done. We find those places and we put a number on them. Because once you can see where it's going, it stops being a mystery and starts being something you can fix.

Check-in: Does that make sense for where you are right now - or is there a specific job or month that's been niggling at you that we should start with?
2. **Step 2 - Sort the team so every decision stops landing on your phone.** You've got a contracts manager and a couple of foremen. But you're still doing half the contracts manager's job because it's quicker. That's not a people problem. It's a structural problem. There's no clear ownership, no accountability, and no way for the team to know what good looks like without checking with you first. We fix that. We build the structure around the people you've already got - job ownership, decision-making, reporting back to you on what matters rather than bringing you everything. Most MDs find within the first few weeks that the calls start to drop off. Not because the lads have changed, but because they finally know what they're supposed to own.

Check-in: When you picture your contracts manager running things without you checking in - does that feel possible, or does it feel a long way off?
3. **Step 3 - Get your time back and build a pipeline you can rely on.** Right now you're pricing reactively. Whatever comes in, you put a number on it when you've got a spare hour, usually on the drive between sites. That means you're winning work on price when you're stretched, losing tenders you should have won because you didn't have time to price them properly, and never quite sure what the next six months looks like. We block time for pipeline. We look at where your best work comes from - which clients, which type of job, which margin - and we build a simple process to get more of it. Not a marketing plan with a logo. A practical approach that fits around the way you actually work. When the pipeline is steady and you can see what's coming in, the pressure at month-end starts to ease.

Check-in: If you could get two days a month back to focus purely on pricing and pipeline, what do you think that would be worth to the business?

### Results and proof

I started my first business at 17. I spent 28 years building and running real businesses before I coached anyone. When I talk about what it feels like to carry everything yourself, it is not a phrase I picked up at a seminar. I have been coaching business owners for 17 years and was ranked the world's number one business coach four years running, from 2015 to 2018. That ranking was measured on client results and business growth delivered, not on how many people signed up. For the last 8 years I have worked specifically with MDs in construction and allied trades. I know what rework costs. I know what a variation that was never written up looks like on the numbers. I know what tight-at-month-end actually means when you've got payroll going out on Friday. John Davy, a business owner I worked with, put it this way: 'I believe that Pam is the best coach I have ever, ever had. Her fees are completely outstripped by the money that you will get back. Specifically I can point to huge amounts of profit in my various companies that Pam and only Pam recognised.' That is the kind of result I work towards with every client.

